    "turn_text": "to believe. that I have, and I would do the right thing. But what I think that we often do, and we don't teach leadership enough to young people, we don't teach, it's actually, especially in modern day liberal New York culture, it's actually, people are against teaching about leaders, right? Everything has to be larger forces. They don't want to think about the individual. And what we cannot do, though, in our conversations about leaders is establish caricatures. And so as we look back at this story, yes, 100%. I like to think that I would stand up. I like to think I have stood up and said, no, now is when we do the hard thing. Now is when we have faith. Now is when we dig deep. But you are still dealing with human beings. That's right. And these were folks that were scared. This story has been spoken about in conversations just like this so much. And often the conversation and the analysis focuses on Moses in this communication with God and it focuses on Aaron's failures. It very rarely focuses on the masses.",
