    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 82329,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82329.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 61,
    "turn_text": "I enlisted after a graduate for the one school of economics. I was the dumbest one in basic training, the dumbest guy. The only reason why I even got out of basic training is because I was carried through it by people like Ronnie Gunnarud, 19 years old from Alabama. So, yeah, no, we have far too much arrogance in our society today, and it's all arrogance. The second thing, though, that I have learned is it's interesting what humans feel nostalgic for. And they don't feel nostalgic as often for moments of luxury and exuberance and gluttony. They feel nostalgic for moments of solidarity and shared struggle. I don't know one soldier or not many soldiers who don't in some way shape or form misconduct. I don't know many people who don't in some way, shape or form, miss being a part of an intense campaign. I'm sure you've seen it in people with startups, entrepreneurs, the idea of people who are serving the community and all the, you know, you're a leader in so many great organizations, ways in which the humans are inclined to feel, to miss those moments where they really shared in their sacrifice. And we don't think about humans that way. I have so much optimism about human nature. I have so much optimism. And I have so much optimism for the future, not only of this country, but of this world. And every experience that I have had speaks to me.",
